    {
      "role": "hint",
      "content": "\u003cdiv class\u003d\"step_content\"\u003e\n \u003cp\u003eComment on the relationship.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e",
      "figure": null,
      "figures": null
    },
    {
      "role": "step",
      "content": "\u003cdiv class\u003d\"step_content\"\u003e\n \u003cp\u003eThe digits in the negative powers of 2 are the same as the digits in the corresponding positive powers of 5. Similarly, the digits in the negative powers of 5 are the same as the digits in the corresponding positive powers of 2.\u003c/p\u003e\n \u003cp\u003eThe reason this works for 2 and 5 (and no other numbers, by the way) is that we use a \u003ci\u003edecimal\u003c/i\u003e or base 10 number system, and 2 and 5 are factors of 10, with 2(5) \u003d 10. If you have studied other bases, such as base 12, you might want to experiment to see if you can find a similar pattern there!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e",
      "figure": null,
      "figures": null
    }
  ]
}